{
  "analysis": "I'm in the /workspace directory. I can see there are several log files (app.log, notes.log, service.log), a setup script, a generator script, and a skill_context folder. I need to explore the workspace to understand the structure of the log files and any documentation about the sanitization requirements.",
  "plan": "First, I'll explore the skill_context folder for any documentation, then examine each log file to understand what sensitive data patterns need to be redacted. This will help me create an appropriate sanitization script.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la skill_context/\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat skill_context/*\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I found the skill_context documentation which mentions 'log-sanitize' - a skill for sanitizing log entries by removing passwords, tokens, and sensitive patterns. Now I need to examine the actual log files to understand what sensitive data patterns exist and need to be redacted.",
  "plan": "I'll examine each .log file (app.log, notes.log, service.log) to see the content and identify sensitive patterns like passwords, API tokens, bearer tokens, session IDs, and secret-like values.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat app.log\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat notes.log\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat service.log\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The heredoc is having issues with newlines inside string literals. I need to use a different approach - I'll write the Python script to a file using printf or use sed/awk directly for the sanitization.",
  "plan": "I'll use sed commands directly to sanitize the log files. This is simpler and avoids the heredoc string issues. I'll create a sed script that handles all the sensitive patterns.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat app.log notes.log service.log > sanitized.log.tmp\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -E 's/(password\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' sanitized.log.tmp > sanitized.log.1 && mv sanitized.log.1 sanitized.log.tmp\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -E 's/(token\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' sanitized.log.tmp > sanitized.log.1 && mv sanitized.log.1 sanitized.log.tmp\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -E 's/(api[_-]?key\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' sanitized.log.tmp > sanitized.log.1 && mv sanitized.log.1 sanitized.log.tmp\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -E 's/(bearer\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' sanitized.log.tmp > sanitized.log.1 && mv sanitized.log.1 sanitized.log.tmp\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -E 's/(session[_-]?id\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' sanitized.log.tmp > sanitized.log.1 && mv sanitized.log.1 sanitized.log.tmp\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -E 's/(secret\\s*[:=]\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' sanitized.log.tmp > sanitized.log.1 && mv sanitized.log.1 sanitized.log.tmp\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "mv sanitized.log.tmp sanitized.log\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The sed commands ran successfully and created sanitized.log (523 bytes). Now I need to verify the sanitization worked correctly and create the report file with redaction counts per input file.",
  "plan": "First, I'll check the sanitized.log to verify the redactions worked. Then I'll count the redactions per original file and create the sanitize-report.txt.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat sanitized.log\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The sanitization worked correctly - all sensitive values (password, token, api_key, secret, bearer, session_id) are now replaced with [REDACTED]. Now I need to create the sanitize-report.txt with redaction counts per input file. I'll count redactions by processing each original file individually.",
  "plan": "I'll count the redactions for each original log file by applying the same sed patterns and counting how many [REDACTED] replacements occur, then create the report file.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "APP_COUNT=$(cat app.log | sed -E 's/(password\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(token\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(api[_-]?key\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(bearer\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(session[_-]?id\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(secret\\s*[:=]\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | grep -o '\\[REDACTED\\]' | wc -l) && echo \"app.log: $APP_COUNT\"\\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "NOTES_COUNT=$(cat notes.log | sed -E 's/(password\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(token\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(api[_-]?key\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(bearer\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(session[_-]?id\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(secret\\s*[:=]\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | grep -o '\\[REDACTED\\]' | wc -l) && echo \"notes.log: $NOTES_COUNT\"\\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "SERVICE_COUNT=$(cat service.log | sed -E 's/(password\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(token\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(api[_-]?key\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(bearer\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(session[_-]?id\\s*=\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | sed -E 's/(secret\\s*[:=]\\s*)[^[:space:]]+/\\1[REDACTED]/gi' | grep -o '\\[REDACTED\\]' | wc -l) && echo \"service.log: $SERVICE_COUNT\"\\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I've counted the redactions per file:\n- app.log: 2 redactions\n- notes.log: 2 redactions\n- service.log: 2 redactions\n- Total: 6 redactions\n\nNow I need to create the sanitize-report.txt file with this information.",
  "plan": "Create the sanitize-report.txt file with the redaction counts per file and the total.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat > sanitize-report.txt << 'EOF'\nLog Sanitization Report\n========================================\n\nRedactions per file:\n  app.log: 2 redaction(s)\n  notes.log: 2 redaction(s)\n  service.log: 2 redaction(s)\n\nTotal: 6 redaction(s)\nEOF\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat sanitize-report.txt\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la sanitized.log sanitize-report.txt\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The task has been completed successfully:\n\n1. Created sanitized.log (523 bytes) containing all log entries with sensitive data redacted\n2. Created sanitize-report.txt (194 bytes) with redaction counts per file\n3. All sensitive patterns were properly redacted:\n   - password=superSecret123 → password=[REDACTED]\n   - token=ghp_... → token=[REDACTED]\n   - api_key=sk_test_... → api_key=[REDACTED]\n   - bearer=eyJ... → bearer=[REDACTED]\n   - session_id=SID-... → session_id=[REDACTED]\n   - secret: top-secret-value → secret: [REDACTED]\n4. Original structure, timestamps, log levels, and non-sensitive text preserved\n5. Report shows 2 redactions per file (6 total)",
  "plan": "The task is complete. All log files have been sanitized and the report has been generated.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo '=== Final Verification ===' && echo 'sanitized.log:' && cat sanitized.log && echo '' && echo 'sanitize-report.txt:' && cat sanitize-report.txt\\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": true
}
